(c) all income arising to any person by virtue of a settlement or disposition whether revocable or not and whether effected before or after the commencement of the Indian Income tax
(Amendment Act, 1939
from assets remaining the property of the settlor or disponer, shall be deemed to be income of the settlor or disponer and all income arising to any person by virtue of a revocable transfer of assets shall be deemed to be income of the transferor:
Provided that for the purposes of this clause a settlement, disposition of transfer shall be deemed to be revocable if it contains any provision for the retransfer directly or indirectly of the income or assets to the settlor, disponer or transferor, or in any way gives the settlor, disponer or transferor a right to reassume power directly or indirectly over income or assets;
Provided further that the expression 'settlement or disposition shall for the purposes of this clause include any disposition, trust covenant, agreement or arrangement and the expression 'settlor or disponer in relation to a settlement or disposition shall include any person by whom the settlement or disposition was made;
